--TEST--
A `?T` parameter of an anonymous function is checked against `T` or `null` when a Core\Arr callback calls it, and only `mixed` admits every argument — `rule:types/callable-signature`, `rule:types/arrays`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The bound is asserted on both sides. A call through a bare `callable` checks
// each argument against the parameter's whole declared type, so a `?T` admits a
// `T` and `null`, and refuses the rest exactly as its bare twin `T` does. Each
// declaration below is written twice, bare and nullable, over the same member
// and the same argument. A check that read only the representation would admit
// every nullable half; a check that had been dropped would admit everything.
//
// The keys are `rule:types/arrays`'s `string`, over the packed list the
// neighbouring `arr-a-callback-key-is-a-string-on-a-list-too` pins, so every
// refusal below is a `string` argument meeting a parameter of some other type.
//
// Every question is asked through an option bag's `{by:}`, which is a bare
// `callable`. A member whose callback is a whole parameter writes its
// signature (`rule:types/callable-signature`) and answers these before the run.

array<int> $list = [10, 20, 30, 40];
array<int> $map = ["a" => 1, "b" => 2];

// One character per declaration, in pairs: the bare form then its nullable
// twin. `y` is "the member ran", `n` is "the call threw `LogicError`".
var $marks = "";
try { Core\Arr::unique($list, {by: fn(int $v, int $k) => true}); $marks = $marks . "y"; }
catch (LogicError $e) { $marks = $marks . "n"; }
try { Core\Arr::unique($list, {by: fn(int $v, ?int $k) => true}); $marks = $marks . "y"; }
catch (LogicError $e) { $marks = $marks . "n"; }
try { Core\Arr::unique($list, {by: fn(int $v, bool $k) => true}); $marks = $marks . "y"; }
catch (LogicError $e) { $marks = $marks . "n"; }
try { Core\Arr::unique($list, {by: fn(int $v, ?bool $k) => true}); $marks = $marks . "y"; }
catch (LogicError $e) { $marks = $marks . "n"; }
try { Core\Arr::unique($list, {by: fn(int $v, array<string> $k) => true}); $marks = $marks . "y"; }
catch (LogicError $e) { $marks = $marks . "n"; }
try { Core\Arr::unique($list, {by: fn(int $v, ?array<string> $k) => true}); $marks = $marks . "y"; }
catch (LogicError $e) { $marks = $marks . "n"; }
// The pair the key's own type is on: both halves accept.
try { Core\Arr::unique($list, {by: fn(int $v, string $k) => true}); $marks = $marks . "y"; }
catch (LogicError $e) { $marks = $marks . "n"; }
try { Core\Arr::unique($list, {by: fn(int $v, ?string $k) => true}); $marks = $marks . "y"; }
catch (LogicError $e) { $marks = $marks . "n"; }
// `mixed` admits every argument and has no bare twin.
try { Core\Arr::unique($list, {by: fn(int $v, mixed $k) => true}); $marks = $marks . "y"; }
catch (LogicError $e) { $marks = $marks . "n"; }
echo $marks, "\n";

// The same pair in the *value* position, so the rule is about an argument
// rather than about the key: an `int` element meeting a `string` parameter.
var $value = "";
try { Core\Arr::unique($list, {by: fn(string $v, string $k) => true}); $value = $value . "y"; }
catch (LogicError $e) { $value = $value . "n"; }
try { Core\Arr::unique($list, {by: fn(?string $v, string $k) => true}); $value = $value . "y"; }
catch (LogicError $e) { $value = $value . "n"; }
echo $value, "\n";

// An admitted argument arrives intact: each key reaches a `?string $k` whole.
echo Core\Str::join(Core\Arr::keys(Core\Arr::countBy($list, {by: fn(int $v, ?string $k) => $k as string})), ","), "\n";
echo Core\Str::join(Core\Arr::keys(Core\Arr::countBy($map, {by: fn(int $v, ?string $k) => $k as string})), ","), "\n";

// The refusal names the argument and what was given.
try {
    Core\Arr::countBy($map, {by: fn(?bool $v, mixed $k) => $k as string});
} catch (LogicError $e) {
    echo $e->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
nnnnnnyyy
nn
0,1,2,3
a,b
argument 1 to a `callable` does not accept an int
